2 CONT Newspaper transcriptions by Mark Christian, sources as noted.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Roland Record, Roland, Story County, Iowa, Wednesday, March 21, 1934.
2 CONT
2 CONT "MRS. LURA DIED YESTERDAY
2 CONT
2 CONT Miss Bertha Lura passed away last evening at the home of her sister, Mrs. John Evenson, following a few hours illness. She suffered a stroke yesterday afternoon and her illness ceveloped immediately into pneumonia, which caused he
2 CONC r death shortly before midnight. The funeral will be held Friday afternoon at 1:30 at the home and at 2:00 o'clock in Salem church."
2 CONT
2 CONT The Roland Record, Roland, Story County, Iowa, Wednesday, March 28, 1934.
2 CONT
2 CONT "FUNERAL FOR OLD RESIDENT
2 CONT
2 CONT BERGITTE LURA HAD LIVED IN COMMUNITY FOR OVER HALF CENTURY
2 CONT
2 CONT Following is the obituary of Bergitte Lura, an old resident of the community, whose funeral was held last Friday.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bergitte, youngest daughter of Haaver and Bertha Lura, was born at Lura, near Stavanger, Norway, on October 19, 1866, and passed away on March 20, 1934, at the age of 67 years and five months.
2 CONT
2 CONT When she was 14 years of age, she, with her widowed mother, came to this county to the home of her sister, Mrs. John Evenson. She was confirmed shortly after by Rev. Solberg in the Salem church. She spent most of her life in the v
2 CONC icinity of Roland, with the exception of several years spent at Marshalltown and Minneapolis.
2 CONT
2 CONT She had suffered from a hip ailment for the last 15 years, a disease which had gradually become worse, making it difficult for her to get about. She suffered a slight stroke a year and a half ago, and had been in poor health since
2 CONC , although apparently she was feeling well Tuesday afternoon when she suffered another stoke which caused her death just before midnight.
2 CONT
2 CONT She leaves to mourn her passing, two sisters, Mrs. John Evenson of Roland, with whom she had made her home, and Mrs. M. Malmin of McCallsburg, and one sister-in-law, Mrs. H. H. Lura of Roland, besides 26 nieces and nephews and oth
2 CONC er relatives.
2 CONT
2 CONT The funeral was held on Friday afternoon, a brief service in the home at 1:30, and at 2:30 in the Salem church, with Rev. Olaf Holen officiating. Mr. and Mrs. O. S. Boyd sang two numbers, "The Old Rugged Cross," and "Den Store Hvi
2 CONC de Flok." Rev. Holen spoke in the English language and also made a few remarks in the Norwegian language. The body was laid to rest in the Roland cemetery. Pallbearers were nephews of the deceased, namely, Harry, Benford and Orvil
2 CONC le Evenson and Victor Lura, and Gerhard Malmin.
2 CONT
2 CONT Those from out of town in attendance at the funeral were Mr. and Mrs. Sam Swanson, Mr. and Mrs. A. R. Cory, Mr. and Mrs. Orville L. Evenson, and Mrs. Carl Johnson of Marshalltown; Mr. and Mrs. George Legvold of Des Moines; Mr. and
2 CONC  Mrs. B. W. Evenson and Peter Skogerboe of Story City; Mr. and Mrs. Victor Lura of Gilbert; Mrs. M. Malmin, Gerhard Malmin, Mrs. O. V. Olson, Mr. and Mrs. Peter Egenes, Ole Hougen, Maurice Hougen and Hazel Hougen, Mr. and Mrs. Geo
2 CONC rge Vallem, Mr. and Mrs. Lewis Lura, Mrs. Amos Hanson, of McCallsburg, Miss Bertha Lura of Ames, and Miss Theresa Evenson of Council Bluffs."
